{"name":"Covered Bridge","hex":"#6a3c3b","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"covered bridge","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A bridge with a (usually wooden) house-like structure covering it; in many of the wooden versions, the roof and siding protect the structural timbers from the elements."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/covered_bridge"}],"entries":[{"title":"Covered bridge","blocks":[{"text":"A covered bridge is a timber-truss bridge with a roof, decking, and siding, which in most covered bridges create an almost complete enclosure. The purpose of the covering is to protect the wooden structural members from the weather. Uncovered wooden bridges typically have a lifespan of only 20 years because of the effects of snow and rain, but a covered bridge can last over 100 years. In the United States, only about 1 in 10 survived the 20th century. The relatively small number of surviving bridges is due to deliberate replacement, neglect, and the high cost of restoration."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Larrys_Creek_Covered_Bridge.JPG/330px-Larrys_Creek_Covered_Bridge.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Covered_bridge"},{"title":"Covered Bridge (Cedarburg, Wisconsin)","blocks":[{"text":"The Covered Bridge in Cedarburg, Ozaukee County, Wisconsin, United States, is one of the last remaining covered bridges in that state, which once had about 40 covered bridges. Built in 1876 to cross Cedar Creek, the bridge is 120 feet (37 m) long and is made of pine with oak lattices. It was listed on the National Register of Historic Places in 1973 and is now used only for pedestrian traffic."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/Covered_Bridge_Cedarburg_WI_May-09.jpg/330px-Covered_Bridge_Cedarburg_WI_May-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Covered_Bridge_(Cedarburg%2C_Wisconsin)"},{"title":"Bridge","blocks":[{"text":"A bridge is a structure designed to span an obstacle, such as a river or railway, allowing vehicles, pedestrians, and other loads to pass across. Most bridges consist of a flat deck, supported by beams, arches, or cables. These structures rest on a foundation that is carefully designed to transfer the weight of the bridge to the subsoil without settling."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Bridge_Alcantara.JPG/330px-Bridge_Alcantara.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bridge"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Spreuerbr%C3%BCcke_in_Luzern_20190421.jpg/500px-Spreuerbr%C3%BCcke_in_Luzern_20190421.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Spreuerbr%C3%BCcke_in_Luzern_20190421.jpg/1280px-Spreuerbr%C3%BCcke_in_Luzern_20190421.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Spreuerbr%C3%BCcke_in_Luzern_20190421.jpg/500px-Spreuerbr%C3%BCcke_in_Luzern_20190421.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Spreuer Bridge in Lucerne","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASpreuerbr%C3%BCcke_in_Luzern_20190421.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Schuylkill_Permanent_Bridge_at_High_Street%2C_the_first_covered_bridge_in_America.jpg/500px-Schuylkill_Permanent_Bridge_at_High_Street%2C_the_first_covered_bridge_in_America.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Schuylkill_Permanent_Bridge_at_High_Street%2C_the_first_covered_bridge_in_America.jpg/1280px-Schuylkill_Permanent_Bridge_